    {
      "id": "R8-WF02",
      "file": "02-task-lifecycle",
      "title": "How does one task reach a reviewed outcome?",
      "description": "The proposed P1 task loop uses adaptive analysis, dependency-ready child work and admitted execution attempts. Simple work runs directly. Exceptions retain ownership until stop acknowledgement or fencing; reviewed external actions record observed receipts. Successful plans can later become P3 workflows with optional P4 CI targets.",
      "width": 1000,
      "height": 1480,
      "legend": [
        "progress",
        "control",
        "context"
      ],
      "notes": [
        "Coordinator owns task, plan and dispatch; executor owns processes, workspace and observed evidence.",
        "Completion, acceptance, delivery and settlement remain separate. This is proposed behavior, not runtime proof."
      ],
      "sections": [
        {
          "text": "Phases are adaptive: investigate → deep-dive → architect → plan → act → review",
          "x": 50,
          "y": 130
        }
      ],
